        "Anticipated growth in water demand in Port Hedland will require new supply options to be developed. As of now, no climate-resilient solutions have been committed to, and Water Corporation is exploring the option of trucking water in. In its absence, industry has collaborated on a plan for large-scale desalination, but this remains at an early planning stage with no committed delivery mechanism. We score its readiness as 2.",
        "Residents and industry within Port Hedland have their water supplied via Water Corporation's Port Hedland Water Supply Scheme, drawing from the De Grey and Yule borefields. Current demand of 13.6 GL/year is expected to rise to at least 21.4 GL/year by 2050 just for community and commercial needs, pushing the borefields, whose total licensed capacity of 20.5 GL/year is already fully allocated, beyond their limit. On top of this, the Department of Water and Environmental Regulation projects industrial demand in the Boodarie Strategic Industrial Area will reach between 19.1 and 54.4 GL/year by 2035, rising to 73–215 GL/year by 2050. Some climate-resilient supply already exists in the region – Fortescue operates a 1.5 GL/year seawater desalination plant at Port Hedland, and Water Corporation contributes 1.8 ML/year of wastewater reuse – but these volumes are negligible relative to projected demand.",
