        "Despite this progress, three structural weaknesses prevent a higher rating. First, the federal R&D portfolio retains a significant allocation to carbon capture, utilisation and storage (CCUS) and carbon offsets, which risks diverting attention and capital from direct emissions reduction technologies that are the only durable pathway to genuine industrial decarbonisation. The Climate Change Authority's Sector Pathways Review has called explicitly for targeted R&D programs for hard-to-abate high-emission sectors, a direction BZE strongly endorses through challenge-based programs and concessional financing mechanisms tailored to sectoral decarbonisation needs. Second, the commercialisation and scale-up pathway between federally-funded pilot research and industrial-scale deployment remains underdeveloped. Kwinana operators consistently identify the \"missing middle\", the transition from demonstration to first commercial plant, as the phase where federal support is least structured and where competing jurisdictions (notably the United States, European Union, Japan, and South Korea) now offer materially stronger incentives. Third, international engagement in federal R&D remains largely bilateral and project-specific; Australia has yet to embed itself as a core node in the emerging global green industrial research architecture, despite the natural fit between Kwinana's resource base, export orientation, and the R&D priorities of major trading partners.",
