        "The federal government's procurement framework is assessed as a 3, reflecting moderate but uneven support for using Commonwealth purchasing power to advance zero-emissions outcomes in Kwinana and comparable industrial precincts. Core policy instruments have transitioned from proposal to active implementation over the past eighteen months, establishing a credible foundation on which to build. However, consistent application across government agencies, the generation of durable demand-side certainty, and the operational readiness of local supply chains to capture the opportunity remain works in progress.",
        "The federal procurement architecture has strengthened materially since BZE's 2025 Hunter and Gladstone assessments. The Environmentally Sustainable Procurement (ESP) Policy moved into its expanded phase on 1 July 2025, broadening its scope beyond construction services to include Information and Communication Technology, textiles, and furniture procurements above $1 million. Suppliers under these categories are now required to meet defined sustainability criteria, including emissions reduction, recycled content, and circular economy measures, and to report publicly against those criteria. The Future Made in Australia (FMiA) Act, supported by the Buy Australian Plan and the National Interest Framework, provides the overarching strategic rationale for using federal purchasing to build domestic industrial capability in priority clean energy and critical minerals sectors. The Capacity Investment Scheme has been streamlined to a single-stage tender and now embeds social outcomes and First Nations participation as explicit merit criteria, broadening the definition of value for money beyond lowest-cost considerations. Together, these instruments directly address the structural gap BZE identified in Make It Here: Building Australia's Clean Industrial Future, specifically the under-use of demand-side levers to complement supply-side grants and concessional financing.",
        "For Kwinana, the stakes of effective federal procurement are exceptionally high. The precinct hosts several of the assets that will determine whether Australia's heavy industry can decarbonise at pace: Alcoa's Kwinana alumina operations (now in transition following the refinery's 2024 closure announcement and redeployment planning), the BlueScope NeoSmelt electric smelting furnace feasibility at the Kwinana Industrial Area, CSBP's ammonia and chemicals complex, and the Australian Marine Complex at Henderson, a nationally significant shipbuilding and defence industrial hub with direct exposure to Commonwealth procurement. Federal procurement of green aluminium, green iron and steel, low-carbon cement, green ammonia, and zero-emissions maritime vessels would translate policy intent into durable local demand for precisely the products Kwinana is positioned to supply. The production tax credits announced for green aluminium and critical minerals refining, while strictly an investment-framework instrument, are best understood as complementary to procurement: credits reduce the cost of producing zero-emissions materials, and procurement guarantees a floor of domestic demand for them.",
        "Three structural weaknesses prevent a higher rating. First, implementation consistency across federal agencies remains uneven. The ESP Policy applies to a defined subset of contract categories above defined thresholds; it does not yet function as a whole-of-government procurement framework, and enforcement mechanisms, including penalties for non-compliance and independent audit, are underdeveloped. Second, the framework has not yet generated the demand-side certainty needed to unlock first-of-a-kind industrial investment. Commonwealth procurement has not mandated that major federally funded infrastructure, including transmission, rail, defence, housing, and public buildings, must specify locally manufactured, low-emission materials such as green steel, green cement, green aluminium, and green ammonia, even where domestic supply is technically feasible. The absence of this \"guaranteed offtake\" signal is a decisive gap for proponents in Kwinana evaluating final investment decisions on projects such as green iron from Pilbara ores, green ammonia from Kwinana-based hydrogen, and critical minerals refining. Third, supplier-side capacity support for small and medium enterprises, and in particular for First Nations-owned businesses in the South West and Pilbara supply chains, has not been scaled to match the expanded reporting and compliance expectations. Without targeted capability-building programs, the benefits of the new procurement architecture risk accruing disproportionately to large incumbent suppliers already able to meet sophisticated reporting requirements, narrowing rather than broadening the regional industrial base.",
        "For the federal procurement framework to achieve a Strong Support rating, three coordinated actions are required. First, the Commonwealth should establish binding minimum local-content and embodied-emissions requirements for all federally funded major infrastructure, transport, defence, and housing procurement –- with explicit carve-outs for green steel, green aluminium, green cement, green ammonia, and critical minerals –- to convert policy intent into durable demand signal. Second, the ESP Policy should be extended to cover all federal procurement above a defined threshold (irrespective of category), backed by transparent reporting, independent audit, and enforceable penalties for non-compliance. Third, a dedicated supplier capability program should be funded to support SMEs and First Nations businesses in the Kwinana, Pilbara, and South West supply chains to meet sustainability and reporting requirements, with direct alignment to the WA Government's Local Content Policy and the Green Energy Approvals Initiative's equity partnership criteria. Implemented together, these measures would position Commonwealth procurement as a primary driver of Kwinana's industrial transition rather than a residual support mechanism.",
