        "The Western Australian Government's emissions reduction commitment is scored as a 2. This reflects comprehensive sectoral planning, a binding state requirement for government trading enterprises to develop net zero transition plans, and emerging First Nations-led renewable energy partnerships, offset by the absence of a legislated whole-of-economy target, a heavy reliance on federal levers, and a regional grid with negligible renewable generation and no firm pathway to decarbonise the gas-fired capacity that new heavy industry continues to lock in.",
        "Western Australia remains the only Australian jurisdiction without a legislated emissions reduction target, and the only state where emissions are still actively rising over the long term. While emissions have plateaued in recent years around 89.4 Mt CO2-e (roughly 17% above 2005 levels), this stability hides a significant gap. Aligning with a 1.5°C pathway requires a 75% reduction below 2005 levels by 2035, necessitating rapid, legally binding action. The Climate Action this Decade Bill 2025 includes a 2030 target, but its passage through Parliament remains unconfirmed.",
        "Despite this legislative gap, the Pilbara faces an even more concentrated version of the state's structural challenge: the region contributes more than 40% of WA's total emissions while producing around 98% of the nation's iron ore exports and roughly a fifth of the state's economic output. The Sectoral Emissions Reduction Strategy (SERS) addresses this through sector-specific pathways, imposing declining baselines on heavy industry – including LNG and iron ore – via the federal Safeguard Mechanism, which has applied to facilities emitting more than 100,000 tonnes of CO2-e per year since July 2023. The state is also pulling direct local levers: government trading enterprises are required to prepare and implement net zero transition plans, and Pilbara Ports Authority, which operates the Port of Port Hedland, has committed under its own Climate Action Plan 2025–2030 to cut Scope 1 and 2 emissions by 80% by 2030 against a 2020 baseline – in line with the State Government's own-operations target – supported by a renewables feasibility study with Horizon Power for solar and battery storage at the port's East Side and Utah Point sites.",
        "However, this reveals a critical tension distinct from the coal-to-gas backfill risk identified in the South West: the North West Interconnected System that serves Port Hedland and the wider Pilbara is already overwhelmingly gas-fired, contains no coal-fired generation, and draws under 2% of its supply from renewables. Pilbara Ports Authority's own transition plan notes it is still waiting for renewable power purchase agreements to become available in the region before it can decarbonise its electricity supply. Meanwhile, major new industrial investment is proceeding on this same gas-dependent grid: in April 2026 the State Government granted final approval for the approximately $4 billion POSCO/Marubeni/China Steel hot briquetted iron plant at Boodarie, near Port Hedland, which will use gas-based direct reduction technology that is less emissions-intensive than a blast furnace but still locks in additional long-term gas demand. As in the South West, SERS leans on the federal Safeguard Mechanism rather than enforceable state-based compliance to drive abatement from these emitters – a structural weakness that carries proportionally more weight in the Pilbara, given the region's outsized share of the state's industrial emissions.",
        "The state has deployed some financial incentives directly relevant to the region, including the $1 billion Strategic Industries Fund, which allocated $27.1 million in the 2026–27 State Budget to water supply studies and infrastructure corridor design for the Boodarie Strategic Industrial Area, and the Pilbara Energy Transition Plan's four priority energy corridors, which aim to connect heavy industry to renewable generation and unlock part of WA's allocation of up to $3 billion in concessional Commonwealth financing under Rewiring the Nation. Social licence efforts are comparatively less developed in the region: PoweringWA's flagship Community Benefits Guideline currently applies only to large-scale renewable projects within the South West Interconnected System, with no equivalent state-backed framework yet extended to the Pilbara. Engagement here instead relies on individual proponent-led agreements, such as the Yindjibarndi Aboriginal Corporation's recent agreement enabling up to 3 gigawatts of Traditional Owner-led renewable development with 25–50% equity participation – a promising model, but not yet replicated as a matter of state policy. As with Kwinana, without an independent statutory body like the New South Wales Net Zero Commission, both accountability and engagement remain ad hoc and project-based, lacking a continuous, legally mandated feedback mechanism.",
        "To achieve a higher score, Western Australia must pass the Climate Action this Decade Bill 2025 with whole-of-economy interim targets and establish an independent oversight body with statutory authority to monitor progress and mandate ongoing engagement. For the Pilbara specifically, extending PoweringWA's Community Benefits Guideline and comparable social licence mechanisms beyond the South West Interconnected System, and ensuring renewable generation and power purchase agreements become available on the North West Interconnected System fast enough to keep pace with new industrial gas demand, will be essential to closing the region's wider commitment gap.",
