        "Western Australia has made a structured, policy-integrated commitment to workforce development. The WA Renewable Hydrogen Strategy 2024–2030 explicitly identifies a skilled workforce as a key enabler of the state's clean energy ambitions, underpinned by the broader WA Climate Policy and Sectoral Emissions Reduction Strategy. The Future State prospectus sets out nine targeted diversification opportunities, including renewable hydrogen and advanced critical minerals processing, which are explicitly linked to the workforce strategy. To support this, WA's electricity workforce projections to 2050 identify trades and technicians, particularly electricians and construction labourers, as being in high demand. Aboriginal empowerment is embedded as a core enabler through equity partnership criteria in the Green Energy Approvals Initiative. This is particularly apparent in the Pilbara, with First Nations-owned energy corporations now developing renewable projects.",
