        "Recent reforms to Western Australia's approvals framework for clean energy projects have cleared bottlenecks and drastically reduced determination timelines for many projects. Continued improvement could be delivered by ironing out inconsistent application across complex projects, removing hidden delays in the pre-lodgement phase and introducing a statutory emissions reduction test. We score its approvals process readiness as a 4.",
        "Clean energy projects in Western Australia may require approvals under two distinct state-level frameworks: environmental assessment under the Environmental Protection Act 1986, and development approval under the Planning and Development Act 2005. Since 2023, the state has systematically reformed both tracks to remove bottlenecks. The Environmental Protection Amendment Act 2024 introduced parallel decision making, allowing proponents to advance planning and ancillary approvals while environmental assessment remains underway. The Coordinator General, formalised under the State Development Act 2025, now functions as a central authority to resolve cross-agency bottlenecks for projects of state significance. The EPA has been repositioned toward a risk-based model with ministerial objectives that explicitly prioritise the energy transition, supported by a dedicated Green Energy Approvals Initiative team within the Department of Water and Environmental Regulation.",
        "The combined effect is already visible in accelerated project timelines across the south-west, where several large projects progressed from lodgement to approval in three to five months within the last two years. However, all demonstrated examples to date are located in the south-west. This reflects two structural features of the Pilbara that distinguish it from regions where the reformed framework has already been tested. Where projects are sited on mining tenements – which blanket much of the Pilbara – the Mining Act 1978 displaces the need for development approval under the planning system entirely. This removes one layer of the approvals architecture, but also the planning pathway through which some of the reformed framework's efficiency gains operate. The Pilbara also sees fewer but substantially larger projects, many earlier in their development lifecycle, meaning fewer have yet encountered the reformed system at the assessment stage. Whether the framework delivers equivalent acceleration for GW-scale projects with complex tenure, native title, and fauna habitat profiles remains to be demonstrated.",
        "Despite these reforms, Western Australia's planning framework does not reach the highest score for four reasons. First, the pre-lodgement phase remains outside statutory timeframes, commonly absorbing six months to two years before formal assessment commences. In the Pilbara, this window is further extended by tenure and native title dependencies with no equivalent in established industrial precincts. Second, the reforms are applied inconsistently, with more complex or contested developments continuing to experience extended timelines. Third, there is no statutory requirement that weights a project's contribution to emissions reduction as a criterion in approval determinations. Clean energy projects continue to be assessed on environmental impact mitigation rather than net zero alignment. Fourth, a critical structural reform – the Draft Renewable Energy Planning Code – provides a consistent framework for the siting and design of clean energy assets but remains pending, and is not expected to be incorporated into planning schemes until after August 2026.",
        "Beyond the state framework, it is worth noting that even where the EPA declines to assess a project under Part IV, a parallel Commonwealth assessment track can be triggered independently for projects with EPBC-listed threatened species exposure – a gap the existing 2015 bilateral agreement does not close. Fortescue's Turner River Solar Hub illustrates this: the EPA determined in May 2025 that the project did not warrant formal assessment, while the Commonwealth determined it a controlled action two months later, which delayed approval. The April 2026 Memorandum of Understanding between the Cook and Albanese governments signals that this gap is recognised, with a new assessment bilateral targeted for finalisation by the end of 2026.",
        "To secure a higher score, Western Australia should introduce statutory timeframes for the pre-lodgement phase, a formal emissions reduction contribution test as a statutory criterion in approval determinations, and timely finalisation of the Renewable Energy Planning Code, currently not expected to be incorporated into planning schemes until after August 2026. Progressing the new Commonwealth bilateral to an approvals agreement would close the remaining gap in state-federal coordination for projects with EPBC exposure.",
