        "Western Australia's local procurement policy is scored as a 2, holding the Basic Support rating recorded in the Kwinana Regional Readiness Report (April 2026).",
        "This is a state-level indicator: the underlying procurement architecture - the WA Buy Local Policy, the WA Industry Participation Strategy (WAIPS), and the Aboriginal Procurement Policy (APP) - is identical across WA regions, and the rating is held for the same reason it was assigned in the KRRR: procurement is not yet directly linked to the state's emissions reduction targets, even though the foundational policy architecture is strong. What changes for this report is the regional evidence available to illustrate that architecture, and Port Hedland, sitting within the Pilbara, has access to several state procurement mechanisms that operate differently - and generally more favourably - than they do for Kwinana.",
        "WA has well-established procurement policies in place, including WA Buy Local for smaller projects and WAIPS. The WA Industry Link annual report states that since 2018, 86% of government procurement - around $52 billion - was procured through WA businesses, with WAIPS supporting 111,467 jobs. The 2024 WAIPS update references climate change and signals further expansion, particularly in renewable energy delivery, and sustainable procurement guides exist for state agencies. As in the KRRR, there remain no visible incentives or enforceable targets that reduce scope 3 emissions through procurement specifically.",
        "Unlike Kwinana, which sits within the Perth Metropolitan Region and receives no regional price preference under the WA Buy Local Policy, Port Hedland falls within Zone 3 of the policy's prescribed-distance classification (Pilbara, Kimberley, Gascoyne, Mid West, and Goldfields-Esperance, assessed against a 400-kilometre radius). Suppliers tendering for Port Hedland-based government work can access a regional price preference of up to 10% (capped at $250,000) for goods and services contracts, or up to 5% (capped at $500,000 combined) for works contracts - a real, quantifiable financial incentive for local and regional suppliers that simply does not apply to Kwinana-based procurement. The Aboriginal Procurement Policy's Aboriginal Participation Requirements reinforce this regional tilt further: contracts of $5 million or more in specified industries carry an Aboriginal employment target of 10% in the Pilbara and Kimberley, compared with 2% in the Perth metropolitan area (including Kwinana), Peel, Wheatbelt, South West, and Great Southern, and on all statewide contracts. Port Hedland businesses also have access to a dedicated Pilbara Development Commission Local Content Adviser and a regularly updated regional tender-opportunities service, with no equivalent regional development commission service available to Kwinana, which falls outside any RDC boundary.",
        "These regional mechanisms are also producing real, recent outcomes in Port Hedland. Round 8 of the WA Government's Regional Economic Development (RED) Grants, announced 9 April 2026, delivered $846,700 across five Pilbara projects, including funding for Port Hedland's Inline Engineering Services Pty Ltd to upgrade its integrated polyurethane conveyor component processing and curing capability. The 2026 expression of interest for locally manufactured green steel for major government projects, flagged in the KRRR as closing in March, did close as scheduled on 27 March 2026; no outcomes have yet been published. The Government's November 2025 announcement of a forthcoming WAIPS Addendum for steel and local-content directions to Western Power, Synergy, and Horizon Power under the Government Trading Enterprises Act 2023 remains at the announced stage, with no confirmed publication of either instrument as of this report.",
        "The score remains held at 2, rather than moving to 3, for the same structural reason identified in the KRRR: procurement criteria still do not require low-emission suppliers in key procurement areas, which Score 3 requires. The gap is reinforced by an independent finding from the WA Auditor General, who reported in November 2024 that the Department of Finance has not published entity-level performance against the Aboriginal Participation Requirement subcontracting and employment targets - including the 10% Pilbara target most relevant to Port Hedland - because of unresolved concerns about how entities are interpreting and applying them. This is a clear, documented enforcement and transparency shortfall, not merely a hypothetical risk. To move toward Score 3, the WA Government would need to embed enforceable low-emission supplier requirements into WA Buy Local and WAIPS, and resolve the reporting gap the Auditor General identified so that compliance with regional and Aboriginal participation targets can be independently verified.",
