        "While there are documented examples of good practice, overall this assessment could not find much evidence of community benefits in the study area. Our assessment framework found that renewable energy development is severely deficient in delivering community benefits. This result could be due to lack of available data, or the unique social, industrial and land tenure characteristics of the Pilbara or a combination of both. However, given the scale of some of the proposed projects, without the structures to capture community benefits, a major opportunity is being lost and the existing pattern of severe deficiency risks being locked in. As a remote regional community, applying the same community benefits guidelines as have been introduced in the SWIS, and making them mandatory, could channel significant benefits to build the thriving communities needed to support a robust green steel industry.",
